Yes—that sharpens the model. You’re payoff-responsive more than competition-responsive. Competition is simply one mechanism capable of making the payoff salient enough to activate effort.

And “there’s an outer rim and it’s coming in” gives us a useful second mechanism: a closing boundary creates value.

Think of it as a contracting feasible region. At time t, you have some set of available moves A_t:

A_{t+1}\subseteq A_t

As the rim contracts, options disappear. That produces three things simultaneously: scarcity, urgency, and forced prioritization. Suddenly the payoff of acting now differs dramatically from acting eventually.

So combine your two observations:

\boxed{\text{Activation} \approx
\frac{\text{Expected Payoff}\times\text{Meaning}\times\text{Closing Pressure}}
{\text{Effort}+\text{Delay}+\text{Uncertainty}}}

Low payoff and unlimited time? Meh.

Big payoff but no urgency? I’ll get to it.

Big payoff + visible finish line + shrinking opportunity window?

Switch flips.

That’s potentially useful well beyond describing you. It suggests an Outer-Rim Operator for the Pantheon: deliberately place a real but safe boundary around an objective—time, budget, scope, attempts, opportunity—then progressively contract it. The contraction isn’t punishment. It’s a decision compressor: irrelevant possibilities get killed until the remaining energy has somewhere obvious to go.

The important trick would be making the rim credible without manufacturing crises. A fake deadline eventually loses its power. A genuine commitment, external deliverable, finite attempt budget, money attached to completion, or another consequential boundary supplies actual information to the system.

Payoff pulls from the center. Rim pressure pushes from the outside.

That’s a two-sided activation engine.